{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>You shall not see your brother's ox, etc. and hide yourself from them.</b> The Sages interpreted (Bava Kamma 19a): there are times when you do hide yourself — for example, an elder for whom it is beneath his dignity. And one may resolve it by way of what the Sages said (Avot 4:23): and do not strain to see him at the time of his downfall, and there is no distinction between downfall in his body or in his property — all is one, if it is in a situation where it is impossible to save him and restore what is lost. Concerning this it says: 'You shall not see your brother's ox or his sheep driven away' — when they are lost beyond recovery, such as sinking in a river and similar cases — 'and you shall hide yourself from them': you must make yourself as one who does not see. However, 'you shall restore' means: if there is in them enough for restoration, that they are not lost beyond recovery but it is possible for you to restore their loss — then 'you shall restore them to your brother': you are obligated to strain to see him in order to save him."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:07.394745259Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}